another grim day for the met in trouble  this time officers at one of its most  famous police stations and the offensive  text they sent to colleagues  one male officer to another i'm going to  smack her to one female officer i would  happily rape you to another if i was  single i would happily chloroform you  women who complained were told play the  game or stay quiet  other messages were racist and ridiculed  the disabled the officers said it was  all just banter  it was the sort of culture that drove  adam pugh out of the force  it's so historical and it's so deeply  ingrained that i don't really believe  that there is any way to reform that  culture i don't believe that there's a  way to change it certainly not from  within  and ultimately that was why i left so  that i could pursue that change and  speak about that change from the outside  rather than from within we need to move  away from the bad apple narrative and  acknowledge that it's actually the tree  that is rotten  and the tree that is burying bad fruit  and that good  good apples or good well-intentioned  police officers can still be a part of  the problem  the police watchdogs investigator said  the behaviour we uncovered was  disgraceful and fell well below the  standards expected of the officers  involved our investigation showed the  officer's use of banter became a cover  for bullying and harassment colleagues  were afraid to speak out about these  behaviors for fear of being ostracized  demeaned or told to get another job  two officers were fired and others  disciplined  the iopc made 15 recommendations and  ordered the met to tackle underlying  cultural issues there's clearly a small  but a  significant minority of people in the  met who have let us down on many  occasions and the the behaviors on this  occasion are completely deplorable  and the the comments that we've seen  that have been made and come to fruition  as a result of this investigation are  completely offensive and there's no  place for them here in the met  home secretary pretty patel said there  had long been a problem with the met  police culture standards she said must  be raised  martin brunt sky news
 

